# Cloudflare Pages `_redirects`. Lives in public/ so Vite copies it to the root
# of dist/, where Pages reads it. Format: <from> <to> [status]. First match wins.
#
# ###########################################################################
# #  DO NOT ADD  `/*  /index.html  200`  TO THIS FILE.                      #
# ###########################################################################
#
# That single line is what makes happier.dev answer HTTP 200 for every URL that
# does not exist. Verified on 2026-08-08:
#
#   curl -sI https://happier.dev/zzz-nope-123   ->  HTTP/2 200, the homepage HTML
#   curl -sI https://happier.dev/robots.txt     ->  HTTP/2 200, the homepage HTML
#
# That is a "soft 404", and it is actively harmful, not merely untidy:
#
#   * Google classifies soft 404s as a crawl-quality defect and reports them in
#     Search Console. Infinite distinct URLs all returning the same 200 page is
#     the canonical duplicate-content pattern.
#   * It silently breaks every well-known-file convention. /robots.txt returning
#     HTML with a 200 means crawlers parse the homepage as robots directives —
#     which is why this site has effectively had NO robots.txt at all. The same
#     applies to /sitemap.xml, /.well-known/security.txt and /llms.txt: they
#     cannot work until this is fixed. This file and the 404 fix are therefore a
#     prerequisite for every other SEO artifact here, not a polish item.
#   * A mistyped link becomes a real-looking page instead of an honest error.
#
# THE FIX, and why it needs no rule here:
#
#   Cloudflare Pages serves `/404.html` from the build output for any request
#   that matches neither a static asset nor a rule in this file — and it serves
#   it with a real HTTP 404 status. public/404.html supplies that page. So the
#   correct catch-all behaviour is achieved by having NO catch-all rule.
#
#   This site is a single page with no client-side router (src/App.tsx renders
#   one document; navigation is anchor-only, #features / #get-started). Anchors
#   never reach the server. There has never been a reason for the SPA rewrite.
#
# IF A REAL ROUTER IS EVER ADDED: still do not add the catch-all. Prerender each
# route to its own file (dist/agents/index.html, …) so Pages serves each one as
# a genuine 200 asset, add the route to ROUTES in src/routes.tsx so the sitemap
# and the prerender stay in step, and leave /404.html to catch everything else.
# The catch-all trades a correct 404 for a convenience you do not need.
#
# THAT MIGRATION HAS NOW HAPPENED. /agents, /agents/<slug> and
# /vs/claude-code-remote-control are prerendered files, not rewrites, and they
# need no rule here — which is the point. The only rules below are short links
# and aliases for URLs that exist elsewhere in the world.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Short links. These are the only rules that belong in this file: finite,
# explicit, one destination each.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/docs        https://docs.happier.dev/                                      301
/docs/*      https://docs.happier.dev/:splat                                301
/guides      https://guides.happier.dev/                                    301
/guides/*    https://guides.happier.dev/:splat                              301
/app         https://app.happier.dev/                                       301
/github      https://github.com/happier-dev/happier                         301
/discord     https://discord.gg/W6Pb8KuHfg                                  301
/releases    https://github.com/happier-dev/happier/releases                301
/license     https://github.com/happier-dev/happier/blob/main/LICENCE       301
/security    https://github.com/happier-dev/happier/security/policy         301
/self-host   https://docs.happier.dev/deployment/self-host-runtime          301

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Aliases for real pages. These are shapes people (and competitors' sitemaps)
# already use for this kind of content, and each would otherwise land on a
# correct-but-useless 404.
#
# `/compare` goes to /agents rather than to a comparison hub, and that is the
# standing answer to "should the two /vs pages have a parent?": a page whose
# only content is two links sits between the query and the answer. The pair
# cross-links directly instead. Revisit when there is a third comparison.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/compare                                 /agents                            301
/alternatives/claude-code-remote-control  /vs/claude-code-remote-control     301
/alternatives/codex-remote               /vs/codex-remote                   301
/vs/codex                                /vs/codex-remote                   301
